{"builder":"Goal: one pull request that meets both acceptance lines of issue https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5 (\"Read TSV too\"):\n1. node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\n2. README.md documents --tsv\n\nContext: bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin and prints JSON.stringify(parse(text)) plus a newline. src/parse.js exports parse(text, sep = ','), which already takes a separator. Tests live in test/csv.test.js and run with `npm test` (node --test).\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check process.argv for `--tsv`. When it is present, call parse with '\\t' as the separator; otherwise keep the current comma behaviour unchanged. Output format (JSON.stringify of the array plus a trailing newline) must stay identical for both modes.\n2. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and asserts the output equals the output of `node bin/csv2json.js` for 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' (i.e. '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'). Keep the existing tests passing.\n3. In README.md, add usage for the flag, e.g. a line `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` with a sentence saying --tsv reads tab-separated input instead of CSV.\n4. Run `npm test` and confirm everything passes; also run by hand: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` and `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` and confirm the two outputs are the same.\n5. Commit and open the pull request referencing issue #5.","verifier":"Check each acceptance line of issue https://github.com/o/csv2json/issues/5 at the pull request's head, in this order. Run the commands from the repository root.\n\nCheck 1 — expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun, for at least two tables (e.g. a 2-column 1-row table and a 3-column multi-row table):\n  printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/tsv.out\n  printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/csv.out\n  diff /tmp/tsv.out /tmp/csv.out\nand likewise with e.g. 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t3\\tx\\nbob\\t4\\ty\\n' versus 'name,age,city\\nann,3,x\\nbob,4,y\\n'.\nPASS: for every table, the --tsv run on the tab-separated version prints a JSON array that is the same as the one the plain run prints for the comma-separated version (parse both outputs as JSON and compare; they must be deep-equal arrays).\nFAIL: either command errors, the --tsv output is not a JSON array, or the two arrays differ for any table (e.g. a single key containing a tab, or wrong values).\n\nCheck 2 — expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRead README.md at the head.\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag and says what it does (reading tab-separated input).\nFAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or mentions it without any explanation of its purpose.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail conditions):\n- Run `npm test`; note whether it passes and whether a test covering --tsv was added.\n- Note whether the outputs in check 1 are byte-identical (diff exits 0), including the trailing newline.\n- Note whether plain CSV behaviour without the flag is unchanged (existing cli test)."}